window.VGRE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "Introduction", "section": "Overview", "text": "VGRE — Virtual GPU RuntimeRun unmodified CUDA on any CPU — no GPU required. Plus a full local-AI stack: train, fine-tune with LoRA, retrieve with an in-tree vector index, and generate — all offline, all dependency-free.Linux verified300/300 testsCUDA ~95%PTX ~95%Zero external vendor lock-inVGRE intercepts CUDA and OpenCL API calls and executes kernels on the CPU using an LLVM ORC JIT, OpenMP + AVX2/AVX-512 SIMD, OS-level unified memory, and an authenticated TCP cluster transport. The same engine ships an in-tree machine-learning stack so you can go from CUDA experiments to real model training "}, {"url": "quickstart.html", "title": "Quick Start", "section": "Overview", "text": "Quick StartFrom clone to a running CUDA kernel on CPU in a few commands.Automated install (Linux / macOS)git clone https://github.com/vgre-org/vgre-runtime.git cd vgre-runtime bash install_local.shinstall_local.sh detects and installs missing dependencies (CMake, LLVM, OpenMP, Flutter), builds the native engine and dashboard, writes ~/.vgre/env, creates your auth token, and links the CLI tools into ~/.local/bin.Open a new terminal, then:vgre-dashboard # launch the real-time monitor vgre-start --test # local master + worker self-testRun a CUDA programBuild your CUDA source as usual, then run it"}, {"url": "installation.html", "title": "Installation", "section": "Overview", "text": "InstallationManual build steps and platform notes.Prerequisites# Ubuntu / Debian sudo apt install -y cmake ninja-build clang llvm-18-dev \\ libomp-dev libssl-dev libsqlite3-dev # Flutter (for the dashboard) — one of: sudo snap install flutter --classic # or: brew install --cask flutterBuildcmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release cmake --build build -j$(nproc) ctest --test-dir build -j$(nproc) # full suite, 300/300 on LinuxSystem requirementsComponentMinimumRecommendedCPUx86-64 (SSE4) or ARM64AVX2 / AVX-512, 8+ coresRAM4 GB16 GB+ (large-model work)OSLinux (glibc 2.31+)Ubuntu 22.0"}, {"url": "running-cuda.html", "title": "Running CUDA Apps", "section": "Overview", "text": "Running CUDA ApplicationsHow VGRE intercepts and executes CUDA on the CPU.Execution modelVGRE parses CUDA-C kernels through a Clang AST, lowers them to LLVM IR, and JIT-compiles to native code with -O3 -march=native. Kernel grids run on an OpenMP thread pool with SIMD vectorization; managed memory is backed by an OS page-fault handler.What worksCUDA Runtime API (~101 functions): memory, streams, events, graphs, textures/surfaces, cooperative launch, CDP.CUDA Driver API (~56 functions): contexts, modules, PTX load + JIT link.cuBLAS (L1/L2/L3, complex), cuDNN (conv/pool/norm/attention/RNN), cuFF"}, {"url": "cluster.html", "title": "Distributed Clusters", "section": "Overview", "text": "Distributed ClustersScale kernels across machines over LAN or WAN.Token setupEvery node in a cluster shares one auth token. Generate it on the master and distribute it to workers:# On the MASTER vgre-token generate # writes ~/.vgre/token, prints SHA-256 fingerprint # On every WORKER (choose one) scp master:~/.vgre/token ~/.vgre/token vgre-token set &lt;paste-token-here&gt; # Verify both sides match vgre-token fingerprintStart the cluster# Master (LAN auto-discovery on) vgre-start --master # Workers on the same LAN vgre-start --worker # Workers over WAN (explicit master address) vgre-start --wo"}, {"url": "dashboard.html", "title": "The Dashboard", "section": "Overview", "text": "The DashboardA real-time Flutter monitor for the running engine.Launch with vgre-dashboard. It loads the native library in-process and polls live telemetry — no mock data. Pages:PageWhat it showsDashboardLive utilization gauges, workload throughput, UVM activity, log stream.Kernel ExplorerJIT-compiled kernels, launch counts, occupancy, per-kernel timing.Cluster TopologyConnected nodes, per-node load, network heatmap.Hardware TuningToggle background compute, block-threads, profiler; set cache model.Memory AnalysisAllocation map, bandwidth stats, UVM page residency.SettingsPolling interval, data"}, {"url": "local-ai.html", "title": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LM", "section": "Overview", "text": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LMA complete offline machine-learning stack built into the runtime — no PyTorch, no FAISS, no external tokenizer library.LoRA fine-tuningvgre.nn.LoRALinear adds trainable low-rank adapters over a frozen base weight, so you can fine-tune on a CPU/laptop with a fraction of the parameters. Adapters merge back to the base for zero-overhead inference.from vgre import nn layer = nn.LoRALinear.from_linear(pretrained, r=8, alpha=16) opt = nn.AdamW(layer.adapter_parameters(), lr=1e-3) # trains A,B only # ... training loop ... layer.merge() # fold adapter into W for fast inference"}, {"url": "debugging.html", "title": "Debugging PTX (GDB)", "section": "Overview", "text": "Debugging PTX with GDBCUDA-GDB-style single-stepping through PTX kernels using a stock gdb — no GPU, no cuda-gdb install.How it worksVGRE ships a PTX single-step interpreter and a GDB remote-serial-protocol server. Each CUDA thread appears as a GDB thread (a lane); the kernel's own virtual PTX registers are exposed to info registers; and breakpoints, stepi, and memory inspection all behave normally in an unmodified gdb.Start the stubvgre_ptx_gdbserver kernel.ptx saxpy 1 4 2000 \\ f32:2.0 buf:f32:4=1,2,3,4 buf:f32:4=10,20,30,40 u32:4 # prints the instruction listing, buffer addresses, and: # gdb"}, {"url": "env-vars.html", "title": "Environment Variables", "section": "Overview", "text": "Environment VariablesWritten to ~/.vgre/env by the installer and loaded automatically.CoreVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_LIB_PATHautoAbsolute path to the shared library.VGRE_LOG_LEVELINFODEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR.VGRE_INSTALL_DIR~/.local/share/VGREInstallation directory.VGRE_WORKER_THREADSnprocOverride worker thread count.VGRE_SIMD_LEVELautoForce SSE4 | AVX | AVX2 | AVX512.VGRE_ENABLE_NUMA0NUMA-aware scheduling (Linux).Cluster / networkingVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_PORT7777TCP port for master and workers.VGRE_CLUSTER_MASTER_ADDRESS—WAN direct connect (IP:PORT / host / [::1]:PORT).VGRE_CL"}, {"url": "api.html", "title": "API Reference", "section": "Overview", "text": "API ReferencePython, C ABI, and the CUDA-runtime shim.Python bindingsThe vgre package wraps the native library via ctypes:SymbolPurposevgre.TokenizerBPE tokenizer; train, load_hf, encode, decode.vgre.LanguageModelIn-tree GPT; train, generate, save, load.vgre.nn.LoRALinearLow-rank adapters; adapter_parameters, merge.vgre.vector.VectorIndexHNSW ANN; add, search, save, load.C ABI (vgre_c_api.h)The stable C surface the wheel and dashboard bind to. Highlights:vgre_init(); vgre_set_profiler_enabled(1); vgre_set_block_threads(0); vgre_get_memory_bandwidth_stats(&amp;stats); // tokenizer vgre_bpe* t ="}, {"url": "cli.html", "title": "CLI Tools", "section": "Overview", "text": "CLI ToolsThe command-line utilities installed with VGRE.CommandPurposevgre-dashboardLaunch the real-time Flutter monitor.vgre-startStart a master or worker node (--master / --worker / --test).vgre-tokenGenerate / set / fingerprint the shared cluster auth token.vgre-discoverPublic-IP discovery and cross-LAN registration.vgre_ptx_gdbserverServe a PTX kernel to a stock gdb for step debugging.gpt2_inferReal GPT-2 inference from safetensors/GGUF (matches Hugging Face).Examplesvgre-start --test # local self-test vgre-token generate # new token + fingerprint vgre-discover --set-master # publish publi"}, {"url": "architecture.html", "title": "Architecture", "section": "Overview", "text": "ArchitectureHow the pieces fit together.LayersInterception — CUDA/OpenCL API shims capture calls at load time.Compilation — Clang AST → LLVM IR → ORC JIT native code, with a persistent disk+memory cache.Execution — OpenMP thread pool + AVX2/AVX-512 SIMD; block/warp scheduling and shared-memory emulation.Memory — UVM via an OS page-fault handler; stream-ordered pools; LZ4-compressed cluster transfers.Distribution — authenticated, encrypted TCP transport; NCCL-style + software-RDMA collectives; tensor/pipeline parallelism and a GSPMD auto-partitioner.ML stack — autograd, BLAS GEMM, tokenizer, sa"}, {"url": "faq.html", "title": "FAQ & Troubleshooting", "section": "Overview", "text": "FAQ &amp; TroubleshootingDoes VGRE need a GPU?No. That is the point — kernels execute on the CPU. A GPU is never required.Is it as fast as a real GPU?No. Expect 10–50× slower for compute-bound kernels; memory-bound kernels are closer with AVX-512. VGRE targets learning, CI, development, and moderate workloads — not latency-critical production inference.Which platforms are supported?Linux x86-64/ARM64 is verified. Windows and macOS are code-complete but not yet CI-verified; treat them as experimental.The dashboard won&#x27;t start / can&#x27;t find the library.Rebuild and reinstall, then open a"}];
